  (note "Last clause: the Greek order is θεὸς ἦν ὁ λόγος, \"God was the Word\". English marks the subject by position and Greek by the article, so the literal layer moves \"the Word\" (with the article) to the front; keeping the Greek order would make \"God\" the subject.")
  (note 14 "θεός without the article, placed first for emphasis: it says what the Word is, not who. Rendering it \"a god\" would supply an indefinite article Greek does not have and that the context (\"the Word was with God\") does not support; \"divine\" would need a different Greek word (θεῖος)."))

